"Resources Scrutiny Committee","time":"5.00 pm","status":"New","webcast":false,"url":"https://democracy.n-kesteven.gov.uk"},{"date":"2026-11-03","committee":"Executive Board","time":"3.00 pm","status":"Confirmed","webcast":false,"url":"https://democracy.n-kesteven.gov.uk"},{"date":"2026-11-04","committee":"Flourishing Communities Scrutiny Committee","time":"5.00 pm","status":"New","webcast":false,"url":"https://democracy.n-kesteven.gov.uk"},{"date":"2026-11-05","committee":"Executive Board","time":"3.00 pm","status":"Cancelled","webcast":false,"url":"https://democracy.n-kesteven.gov.uk"}],"recent":[{"date":"2026-07-20","committee":"Audit Committee","time":"5.00 pm","status":"Confirmed","webcast":false,"url":"https://democracy.n-kesteven.gov.uk"},{"date":"2026-07-23","committee":"Executive Board","time":"3.00 pm","status":"Confirmed","webcast":false,"url":"https://democracy.n-kesteven.gov.uk"},{"date":"2026-09-14","committee":"Resources Scrutiny Committee","time":"5.00 pm","status":"Confirmed","webcast":false,"url":"https://democracy.n-kesteven.gov.uk"},{"date":"2026-09-16","committee":"Flourishing Communities Scrutiny Committee","time":"5.00 pm","status":"Confirmed","webcast":false,"url":"https://democracy.n-kesteven.gov.uk"},{"date":"2026-09-17","committee":"Executive Board","time":"3.00 pm","status":"Postponed","webcast":false,"url":"https://democracy.n-kesteven.gov.uk"}]},"news":[],"news_drafts":[{"headline":"North Kesteven projects \u00a3533k General Fund surplus as borrowing costs fall","lead":"North Kesteven Council is forecasting a \u00a3533,300 surplus on its General Fund for 2026-27, primarily because planned borrowing for its Lafford Homes housing scheme is concentrated in the second half of the year rather than spread throughout, reducing interest payments by \u00a3617,600. The council set a balanced budget in February but Q1 figures show the timing of loan drawdowns has created an unexpected saving. The surplus is being allocated to reserves: \u00a3233,300 to the Our Council Reserve and \u00a3300,000 to the Financial Resilience Reserve. Offsetting the borrowing gain are cost pressures: the Local Government pay award came in at 3.3%, 0.3 percentage points higher than budgeted, costing the council \u00a361,800 across the General Fund and housing account. The council is also absorbing \u00a322,800 in additional fleet fuel costs. The Housing Revenue Account, which manages the council's own housing stock, is projecting a separate deficit of \u00a3160,700 for the year, to be met from reserves.","iso":"2026-09-24","paper_title":"Appendix 1 - Key Performance Indicator Exception Report Quarter 1 2026/27","paper_url":"https://democracy.n-kesteven.gov.uk/documents/s157792/157792.pdf","byline":"PWLBintel \u00b7 from committee papers","draft":true}],"findings":[{"iso":"2026-09-24","committee":"Executive Board","paper_title":"Appendix 1: DRAFT Complaints Performance and Service Report","paper_url":"https://democracy.n-kesteven.gov.uk/documents/s158049/158049.pdf","headline":"Damp and mould complaints (21 cases, 76.2% upheld) triggered Ombudsman-ordered capital remedial works (HHSRS inspection and repairs), creating committed expenditure obligation.","detail":"Damp and mould complaints totalled 21 cases (9% of 217 complaints), with 76.2% upheld (16 of 21). One Ombudsman determination (May 2025) specifically concerned damp, mould and complaint handling. The Ombudsman 'ordered the Council to undertake a full HHSRS inspection, complete any identified works' alongside \u00a3950 compensation. The high uphold rate combined with regulatory (HHSRS) remedial requirements creates non-discretionary capital expenditure.","category":"demand","matters":"Upheld housing health & safety complaints generate committed capital remedial works; Ombudsman-ordered works are mandatory expenditure obligations.","figures":"21 damp and mould complaints (9% of 217); 76.2% upheld (16 of 21)","quote":"The Ombudsman ordered the Council to undertake a full HHSRS inspection, complete any identified works and make a compensation payment of \u00a3950, all of which were completed within the required timescales.","material":true},{"iso":"2026-09-24","committee":"Executive Board","paper_title":"Appendix 1: DRAFT Complaints Performance and Service Report","paper_url":"https://democracy.n-kesteven.gov.uk/documents/s158049/158049.pdf","headline":"Stage 1 investigation quality shows material weakness: 68.4% of complaints initially not upheld were subsequently upheld at Stage 2 review (13 of 19 cases).","detail":"The paper reports that 13 of 19 complaints not upheld at Stage 1 were overturned to upheld at Stage 2 review. This is presented as evidence of weakness: 'it also highlights the importance of continuing to strengthen Stage 1 investigations to ensure all relevant issues, evidence and customer impacts are fully explored at the earliest opportunity.' The high reversal rate indicates systemic issues in initial investigation quality or completeness.","category":"governance","matters":"High Stage 2 reversal rates drive escalation costs and create material risk of further Ombudsman referrals and compensation liability.","figures":"13 of 19 initially not-upheld complaints reversed to upheld at Stage 2 (68.4%)","quote":"Importantly, 13 of the 19 complaints that were not upheld at Stage 1 were subsequently upheld at Stage 2 (68.4%). This demonstrates the value of the Stage 2 review process as an effective safeguard within complaint handling arrangements.","material":true},{"iso":"2026-09-24","committee":"Executive Board","paper_title":"Appendix 1: DRAFT Complaints Performance and Service Report","paper_url":"https://democracy.n-kesteven.gov.uk/documents/s158049/158049.pdf","headline":"14.3% of Stage 1 complaints (31 cases) breached permitted response timescales, with 60.4% reliance on extension periods indicating resource or process constraints.","detail":"Of 217 Stage 1 complaints, only 25.3% were resolved within the 10-working-day standard; 60.4% required extensions to 20 days; 14.3% fell outside permitted timescales entirely. The paper acknowledges 'reliance on extensions remains significant and continues to be an area for improvement during 2026/27.' Ombudsman determinations identified timescale breaches as maladministration, citing 'delays in responding to service requests' and 'insufficient communication regarding those delays'.","category":"governance","matters":"Timescale breaches create Ombudsman escalation risk and indicate potential resource constraints or process inefficiency, with precedent from two 2025/26 upheld determinations.","figures":"14.3% outside permitted timescales (31 complaints); 25.3% within 10-day standard","quote":"Overall, 25.3% of landlord complaints were resolved within the standard 10 working day timescale, with a further 60.4% responded to within an agreed extension period, resulting in 85.7% of complaints being responded to within permitted Complaint Handling Code timescales. The remaining 14.3% were responded to outside permitted timescales.","material":true},{"iso":"2026-09-24","committee":"Executive Board","paper_title":"Appendix 1: DRAFT Complaints Performance and Service Report","paper_url":"https://democracy.n-kesteven.gov.uk/documents/s158049/158049.pdf","headline":"Two Housing Ombudsman determinations upheld in 2025/26, with findings of maladministration and service failure resulting in \u00a31,300 compensation and Ombudsman-ordered remedial works.","detail":"Of three Ombudsman investigations completed in 2025/26, two were upheld. The first (May 2025) found 'maladministration in the Council's handling of concerns relating to windows and doors, damp and mould, and complaint handling' and ordered 'a full HHSRS inspection, complete any identified works' alongside \u00a3950 compensation. The second upheld determination (July 2025) on anti-social behaviour management found 'maladministration in both the handling of ASB reports and complaint handling' and ordered \u00a3350 compensation.","category":"governance","matters":"Upheld determinations create compensation liability precedent and reveal systemic process failures (delays, record-keeping, communication) indicating governance weaknesses beyond individual complaints.","figures":"2 upheld determinations of 3 investigated; \u00a31,300 total compensation (\u00a3950 + \u00a3350); Ombudsman-ordered HHSRS inspection and remedial works completion","quote":"The Ombudsman found maladministration in the Council's handling of concerns relating to windows and doors, damp and mould, and complaint handling, together with service failure relating to management of concerns about the heating system.","material":false},{"iso":"2026-09-24","committee":"Executive Board","paper_title":"Appendix 1: DRAFT Complaints Performance and Service Report","paper_url":"https://democracy.n-kesteven.gov.uk/documents/s158049/158049.pdf","headline":"Ian Williams Ltd contractor accounted for 87 complaints (40% of all landlord complaints) in 2025/26, a notable increase from prior year, with Q4 volume indicating ongoing service delivery risk.","detail":"The paper reports that 87 complaints cited Ian Williams Ltd, representing 60% of all repairs complaints and 40% of all landlord complaints, described as 'a notable increase from 2024/25'. While the uphold rate has declined from 92% in Q1, volume remained elevated in Q4. The council notes: 'the volume of complaints received during Quarter 4 demonstrates that repairs service delivery and customer experience remain an area of risk and will continue to be subject to close oversight.'","category":"other","matters":"Concentration of 40% of complaints with a single contractor represents material contract performance risk with implications for service continuity, tenant satisfaction, and contract management.","figures":"87 complaints (60% of repairs complaints, 40% of all landlord complaints); Q1 uphold rate 92%","quote":"Of these repairs complaints, 87 specifically cited Ian Williams Ltd, representing 60% of all repairs complaints and 40% of all landlord complaints received during the year. This is a notable increase from 2024/25.","material":true}],"editorial":null,"freshness":{"last_paper_iso":"2026-09-24"},"name":"North Kesteven","daily_post":{"iso":"2026-09-24","since_iso":"2026-08-25","distress":false,"headline":"Damp and mould complaints (21 cases, 76.2% upheld) triggered Ombudsman-ordered capital remedial works (HHSRS inspection and repairs), creating committed expenditure obligation.","lead":"Upheld housing health & safety complaints generate committed capital remedial works; Ombudsman-ordered works are mandatory expenditure obligations.","also":["50 other findings in this window"],"watch":"Executive Board on 2026-09-24","n_findings":51,"n_documents":0,"top_paper_title":"Appendix 1: DRAFT Complaints Performance and Service Report","top_paper_url":"https://democracy.n-kesteven.gov.uk/documents/s158049/158049.pdf","draft":true},"findings_total":82,"news_drafts_total":1}}